{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1302228-a-post-on-the-protean-keyword-that-got-way-too-long","id":1302228,"title":"A post on the Protean Keyword that got way too long","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-04-03T08:31:54-07:00","id":1302228,"title":"A post on the Protean Keyword that got way too long","content":"So the shapeshifing rules in the lunar manuscript are great, and protean effects that encourage you to pick charms that go well with your shapes cement that even more. But there's a lot of protean effects and a lot of animals with a lot of merits and special attacks and it can be hard to keep track of it all, so to try to get a sense of things for myself I took to writing this down, so why not post it. I listed different traits, what animals have those traits, how many charms under what attributes care about those traits, and what significant protean charm effects key to them, so I can try to figure out what a lunar with a given shape is going to be good at beyond what just the animals traits in themselves to.<br /> <br /> This is just going to be animal protean effects tied to specific traits, not the ones that apply based on cultural significance or another characters personal fears and desires. Because that's situational and very comlicated. A result is that this will be quite combat focused. Also for quite a few traits there's just too many animals to list. Also I don't own all of Hundred Devils Night Parade so some animals will probably be left out.<br /> <br /> Predator (too many): Appearance (2)<br /> <br /> -Two Charms that make you scarry make you a bit scarrier or can be activated reflexively on join battle. Not a big deal.<br /> <br /> Camouflage (great cat, venemous snake, beetle, moth, octopus): Appearance (2)<br /> <br /> -The bonus with Clover Can't Be Found is nice, turning dice of penalties into successes lost, but its E4.<br /> <br /> Legendary Size (too many): Charisma (1), Perception (1), Stamina (2), Strength (4)<br /> <br /> -These bonuses are a bit all over the place, this isn't what you are paying to cost to get to legendary size for, you're doing it for the bonuses legendary size give.<br /> <br /> Tail/Tentacle/Flexible Attack (tyrant lizard, pestletail, octopus, elepant, shieldback lizard, siege lizard): Dex (2)<br /> <br /> -Ignore the defense bonus from Full Defense actions with Finding the Needles Eye and punt weapons out to medium range when you disarm them with Weapon Snatching Coils. Nice little boost to a charm thats a must-pick anyway and a big boost to an ok one.<br /> <br /> Pack Hunting (dog/wolf, claw strider): Dex (2), Strength (2)<br /> <br /> -Are you into distraction gambits? Because that's what these protean effect boost. I'm generally not, but if you're a claw strider and someone is riding you, you can use Quicksilver Steed Unity to make them reflexively for 1m once per round with your rider as the primary beneficiary, so that's kind of neat. Pack Hunting is a very powerfull merit on its own though.<br /> <br /> More than 4 limbs (octopus): Dex (4)<br /> <br /> -Multiattacks charms get multiattackier. The protean effects arent that amazing exept for Unhesitant Scorpion Lash (get back the WP you spent launching the multiattack) but these are worthwile charms without protean so even a little boost is quite nice. Shame there isn't much in the way of big multilimbed critters.<br /> <br /> Tiny/Minuscule (too many): Dex (5), Wits (1)<br /> <br /> -Tiny/Minuscule size already makes you hard to spot and hard to hit, and these protean effects amplify that rerolling 2s in addition to 1s on stealth rolls, activating defensive charms after attack rolls, and boosting defense and stealth with Twisting Penumbra Veil to clash an enemy attack with a truly amazing stealth roll that subtracts 2 successes from the competing attack.<br /> <br /> Speed Bonus (too many): Dex (2), Strength (1), Wits (1)<br /> <br /> -Some small bonuses on some movement enhancing charms. Mostly relevant with Quicksilver Steed Unity if you're carrying a rider.<br /> <br /> Flying (too many): Dex (2), Strength (2)<br /> <br /> -Some movement boost (one powerfull but expensive, one situational, one limited to once per scene), and add essence non-charm dice on Swooping Hybroc Death-Strike, but it's a follow-up attack charm so not very impactfull.<br /> <br /> Contortionist (Great Stoat): Dex (1)<br /> <br /> -Escape restraints a bit easier. Eh.<br /> <br /> Ambush Hunter (Cat, Claw Strider, great cat, quoll lion, great stoat): Dex (6)<br /> <br /> -Rushing from concealment, generating initiative with stealth rolls, flurrying attacks with stealth rolls to re-enter concealment (with no penalties), and dealing extra damage on Ambush attacks. Ambush Hunter protean effects are about being a stealth killer.<br /> <br /> Keen Nose/Scent (too many): Perception (5)<br /> <br /> -Lets you sniff out poisons, peoples secrets, and track like a bloodhound. Not huge bonuses but there's quite a few of them.<br /> <br /> Sense-Enhancing (practically all): Perception (2)<br /> <br /> Notice when spirits are near so you can activate Percieving the Hidden World. Reflexively force people out of Stealth with a gambit instead of having to spend an action.<br /> <br /> Echolocation (Dolphin): Perception (1)<br /> <br /> -When you use the echolocation charm, you echolocate a little better. Eh.<br /> <br /> Immense Guardian (Yeddim): Stamina (1)<br /> <br /> -Who are you defending that's so weak enemies can hit them after subracting your parry from their result? Well if they can this helps a little.<br /> <br /> Berserker (Bear, Hellboar): Stamina (4)<br /> <br /> -With how many health levels lunars can get and with 11 of them being -4s, the Berserker merit just on its own is very usefull for them. But the protean effects of the merit just make it all that much sweeter. There's Frenzied Bear Fortification subtracting wound penalties from overwhelming, which makes an otherwise pretty lacklustre charm actually kind of decent, and it also reduces the cost of Wounded But Never Down (bad-but-stackable Iron Skin Concentration) which is nice. But the biggest bonuses come with Relentless Lunar Fury and later at E4 Terrible Wolverine Onslaught, for +1 dice on all physical attribute rolls and +1 success on all attacks over what non-berserkers get, plus the bonus from the Berserker merit itself if you're actually injured. It's a lot of dice to be adding to every attack when you can hit as hard as Lunar Hellboars do.<br /> <br /> Impenetrable Armor (Armored Terror, Pestletail, Shieldback Lizard): Stamina (1)<br /> <br /> -It's one charm, but its really good and makes you even harder to crash.<br /> <br /> Grappling Abilities (too many): Perception (1), Strength (2)<br /> <br /> -Bonus dice to grapple dematerialized spirits and force them to materialize. Extra dice on control rolls. Slam victims into other enemies to do full slam damage to both instead of only doing half to the second guy.<br /> <br /> Venomous (Venomous Snake, Octopus, Wasp, Jellyfish): Stamina (2)<br /> <br /> -Between these protean effects and Enhanced Toxin mutation, you can make some brutal poisons. At E2 octopus venom hits for 4i aggravated with ~10 turn duration for just 3m 2i. Also you can use this poison for an unblockable counterattack if you get injured.<br /> <br /> Crippling Attack (Hellboar, Hippo, River Dragon): Strength (2)<br /> <br /> -An extra die of decisive damage with Prey-Hobbling Bite and lets you get your WP back from Limb-Maiming Flourish (decisive damage booster) if the attack incapacitates or inflicts a crippling injury. Pretty nice.<br /> <br /> Deadly Charge (Iron Skull Devil, Ox-Dragon): Strength (2)<br /> <br /> -Knock back enemies a bit farther with Mighty Ram Smash but doesnt increase max distance. Add non-charm successes to smash through an obscale with Fortress-Smashing Charge. Eh.<br /> <br /> Trample (Hellboar, Ox-Dragon, Siege-Lizard, Elk, Hippo, Shieldback Lizard, Thunder Lizard, Elephant): Strength (4)<br /> <br /> -Some minor bonuses but notably some extra decisive damage on Behemoth's Footprint Thread and World-Shaking Slam.<br /> <br /> Leaping Pounce (Cat, Great Cat): Strength (2)<br /> <br /> -Makes a charm-success on a rush into non-charm with Pouncing Beast Impact. Makes your Mighty Behemoth Leap attack unblockable.<br /> <br /> Quite nice. Proning everyone withing close range when you hit the ground as a tiny cat is pretty hilarous.<br /> <br /> ------<br /> <br /> So which animals benefit the most from protean effects?<br /> <br /> First I'd say Cats for being the only Tiny Ambush Hunter making them perfect stealth assassins, which given that its already been established they're great even without protean effects makes them pretty amazing.<br /> <br /> Second Hellboars, because Berserker is awesome and Hellboars have much better stats than bears (most notably Hellboars can have 10+essence overwhelming with Claws of the Silver Moon + Ferocious Biting Tooth), and between Trample, Keen Nose, a Speed Bonus, and a Crippling Attack they just get so many protean effects.<br /> <br /> Third the vicious mutant tiny land-octopus, with its ridiculous stealth, good defense, and murderous poison. Also the only ones who get those multiattack charm protean bonuses.<br /> <br /> Fourth is claw Strider. Now I've never seen anyone use distract gambits ever I think, but they can't be so terrible that doing them reflexively for 1m with charms that boost them to give the initiative to multiple allies and strip the attacked enemy of equal initiative can be bad. Can they?<br /> <br /> This is not to say other animals aren't amazing. Hippos, Tyrant Lizards, Elephants, and Pestletails f.ex. are all very strong, but that's primarily because of very good statlines and attacks/merits rather than protean bonuses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2579-limaxophobiac","user":"limaxophobiac","date":". 04-03-2019, 12:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2579-limaxophobiac","name":"limaxophobiac","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"294"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-03T08:49:39-07:00","id":1302232,"title":"","content":"Great stuff! Very useful.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-03T09:43:24-07:00","id":1302248,"title":"","content":"Just wanted to say that my Twilight sorcerer has an elemental familiar who's entire design is to do Distract gambits each fight to grant initiative for my spells. It's hard to spam but it's definitely a useful gambit, so I see real benefit for the Claw Striders.<br /> <br /> Thanks for posting this, great breakdown and analysis.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I post Artifacts in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>. How I make them is in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/828820-how-to-write-artifacts-and-evocations\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>.<br /> I have made many tools and other things for 3rd Edition. I now host all of my creations on my Google site: <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/unsungherovault/home\" target=\"_blank\">The Vault of the Unsung Hero</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/368-the-unsung-hero","name":"The Unsung Hero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar368_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2288"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-03T09:44:58-07:00","id":1302249,"title":"","content":"I feel as though Protean is a lot easier to play by ear than Totemic, because you can always go out and get extra shapes.<br /> <br /> My assessment would be less about buying Charms that go well with your shapes and just buy whatever are useful and fit character concept. If you have a shape that can yield a Protean effect from them, great. If you don't, just get it at the first convenience.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}